# Plan writer

Fills the `develop.plan-writer` capability slot. Reads one approved
`spec.md` and writes a draft `plan.md` — it approves nothing; approval is
a separate human step (the engineering lead) recorded in the artifact's
own frontmatter and checked by the gate engine before develop can close
("Approval" below).

## Where the files are

Every gating artifact for this ticket lives in the ticket's own directory
inside its worktree: read the source artifact from `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/`,
write `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/plan.md` next to the `phase-state.json` there,
committed on the ticket branch with the code. **Not the repo root** —
root-level artifacts made every later ticket inherit the previous one's
approvals and made concurrent tickets collide. The gate engine also
checks the frontmatter `id` against `phase-state.json`: another ticket's
id never satisfies a gate.

## Why the plan comes before the code

This mirrors the plan-before-execute mode both Claude Code and Cursor
support natively, and the AI-native SDLC playbook's "Build" step: the
plan is reviewed and approved *before* implementation starts, not after,
as a summary. Approving `plan.md` approves the *approach*; the code still
goes through `pr-review` in launch — two reviews, two questions ("right
approach" vs. "built correctly"), not one duplicated.

## Which source artifact, and what "approved" means per track

`fast-track` and `emergency` skip `define`/`design` by track policy, so
`develop` is reached with no `spec.md` ever written. **Read
`pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/phase-state.json`'s `track` first** and apply the
matching rule; they differ deliberately:

- **standard** — the source is `spec.md`, genuinely approved; the design
  gate had to clear to reach develop at all.
- **fast-track** — the genuinely approved `intent.md`, with
  `problem`/`proposed_outcome` standing in for the spec's fields and
  `skip_reason` for why design was skipped.
- **emergency** — **there is no approved source artifact yet, and
  requiring one here would be wrong.** Fix first, paperwork after: work
  from a draft `intent.md` or the raw incident, without fabricating the
  approval this track deliberately defers.

"Genuinely approved" means all three of `status: approved`, `approved_by`
and `approval_evidence` (see "Approval" below); approved with no evidence
link never cleared the gate. Note in the body which track this was, and
on fast-track/emergency that it went ahead without a formal spec. Detail:
`.claude/skills/plan-writer/references/track-sources.md`.

## Working from an incident, on the emergency track

On `emergency` the source is the raw incident, so the plan is only as
good as the reading of it. This is guidance, **not a precondition** — a
root-cause analysis is not required first, and waiting would invert the
point of the track.

- The same problem through a support ticket, an alert and a customer
  escalation is **one** incident, not three. Deduplicate before planning.
- **Correlation is not causation.** Two incidents close in time don't
  share a cause until evidence says so; until then it is an observation.
- A cause that can't be confirmed from the evidence is `[PENDING]`, not
  the most plausible explanation — which is what an agent produces most
  fluently, and a plan built on it looks identical to one built on
  evidence. Cite the log line, trace, commit or timeline it rests on.

Carry it into the body and `risks_and_rollback`: the incident, the
believed cause and its evidence, what is unconfirmed, which steps are
mitigation rather than fix. Recurrences: `.claude/skills/plan-writer/references/incident-reading.md`.

## spec-kitti (optional path)

In repos that use spec-kitti (the client's Spec Kit fork),
`/spec-kitti.tasks` may already have decomposed this epic into
`specs/<###-feature>/tasks.md`, each task carrying a `- jira: <KEY>`
line. Check first, via Bash:

```bash
node .pdlc/bin/spine.js spec-kitti --ticket BANK-1234
```

If it finds the feature and lists tasks for this ticket, those tasks — in
their order, with their file paths — **are** the `implementation_steps`;
untagged setup tasks are prerequisites only when not yet done (`[x]`
means done). `touched_systems` comes from the task file paths,
`risks_and_rollback` from `plan.md`'s deployment section; cite
`specs/<###-feature>/tasks.md` in `source_docs`. The per-track approval
rule is unchanged: a task list is an input, not an approval. If it
reports no spec-kitti feature, or no `tasks.md`, proceed unchanged.

## What to do

1. Read the source artifact for this ticket's track, per the section
   above: `id`, `title`, and either
   `technical_architecture`/`handoff_documentation` (from `spec.md`) or
   `problem`/`proposed_outcome` (from `intent.md` / the incident).
2. Explore the actual repo/codebase before writing steps — see "Explore
   before you plan" below; a plan grounded in what's really there beats
   one inferred from the spec's prose. Follow this repo's own stack
   conventions — whichever stack skill is loaded this session
   (`.claude/skills/<id>/SKILL.md`, `.cursor/rules/<id>.mdc`).
3. Write `pdlc/<TICKET-ID>/plan.md` with YAML frontmatter matching the
   `plan.v1.json` schema shipped at `.pdlc/schemas/plan.v1.json`: `id`
   and `title` from the source (`id` matching `phase-state.json`),
   `cycle: 1`, ordered and concrete `implementation_steps`, an honest
   `touched_systems` (a track with `escalation` checks the real diff
   against it), `test_strategy`, `risks_and_rollback`, `source_docs`,
   `status: draft` — field by field in
   `.claude/skills/plan-writer/references/frontmatter-fields.md`. On a latency-sensitive path add a
   response-time regression check to `test_strategy`, see below.
4. List anything unresolved as `open_questions` — an approach that
   depends on another team's answer, an ambiguity needing the engineering
   lead's call. Do not invent answers.
5. Stop. Do not set `status: approved` or invent an `approved_by`, and do
   not start the implementation until `plan.md` is approved — the point
   of plan-before-code.

## Explore before you plan

A plan citing paths nobody looked for is how a repo ends up with a second
copy of a service it already had, and the design artifact may be weeks
old. Before writing a step, search the repo for the area this ticket
describes. Four things then hold, expanded in `.claude/skills/plan-writer/references/repo-exploration.md`:

1. **Every path in `implementation_steps` is either a real file or
   module you actually located here, or is explicitly marked `NEW`.**
2. **If the plan creates a new module or service, say explicitly that
   you looked and found nothing equivalent** — that is a scope decision.
3. **Confirm no in-flight implementation would be silently overwritten**
   — another branch, worktree or uncommitted changes — and say so.
4. **If the repo contradicts the source artifact**, that is an
   `open_questions` entry, never a winner you quietly picked.

**Then present the plan and stop**: what you confirmed exists, the files
you intend to touch, the smallest change that does the job. Wait for an
explicit go-ahead before writing code, and report what commands told you
rather than pasting raw output.

## Response-time regression, in `test_strategy`

On a latency-sensitive path, `test_strategy` says which operations get
measured and where, the target threshold, and the baseline — the previous
measurement or `[PENDING]`, never invented. A missing baseline is not a
blocker: evaluate against the threshold alone and say so. Measure
repeatably (p50/p95); a confirmed regression is a blocking finding for
the launch review, named with the endpoint and the magnitude. Method:
`.claude/skills/plan-writer/references/performance-and-estimates.md`.

## Estimation is a human input

Effort and duration come from the people who would do the work. Where a
step needs a size and nobody has given one, record it as `[ASSUMPTION:
<size or range>, derived from <what you based it on>]` — a range or
t-shirt size, never a bare number, never a commitment. Tie the confidence
to what is `[PENDING]`; the engineering lead corrects it at the gate.

## No invention — sourced, `[PENDING]`, or `[ASSUMPTION]`

Every claim in `plan.md` is **sourced** (a real file, module, artifact or
named human — say which), `[PENDING]` (a baseline nobody measured, an
interface nobody agreed — write the marker, add what would settle it to
`open_questions`), or `[ASSUMPTION: …]` (assumed capacity, assumed
ownership). None of the three means invented — worse than a gap.

## Approval

The engineering lead's approval needs all three of these recorded on the
artifact, or the develop gate stays blocked:

- `status: approved`
- `approved_by: <name>`
- `approval_evidence: <link>` — the Jira comment, Slack thread, PR review, or email where the approval actually happened

`require_approval_evidence` is on for this client
(`.pdlc/phase-graph.yaml`), and applies even when the lead says it
straight to you in the session ("looks good, approved") — a name typed
into a file is not proof. With a Jira MCP connection, use the approval
comment's permalink; without one, ask for the link.

## Example output

`pdlc/BANK-4821/plan.md`, shortened — the full version is in
`.claude/skills/plan-writer/references/worked-example.md`.

```markdown
---
id: BANK-4821
cycle: 1
title: Statement export times out for accounts with 10k+ transactions
implementation_steps:
  - "export-api: add job-queue submission path for requests projected over 10k transactions, reusing the existing job-queue client already used by month-end batch"
  - "export-api: add GET /exports/{job_id} polling endpoint (pending/ready/failed)"
  - "statement-service: emit export.completed event on job completion"
touched_systems: [export-api, statement-service]
test_strategy: "Unit tests for the >10k-transaction branch in export-api's request handler (job submitted vs. synchronous path). Integration test for the poll-until-ready flow against the job queue's local/test mode."
risks_and_rollback: "Job queue already carries month-end batch at higher volume, so capacity risk is low. Rollback is a config flag (EXPORT_ASYNC_THRESHOLD) raised past any real account size."
open_questions: ["Polling interval not yet agreed with export-api's owning team — confirm before implementation starts (carried over from spec.md)."]
source_docs: ["https://client-docs.example.com/prd/statement-export-v2"]
status: draft
---

Plan follows spec.md hand-off documentation; no new services.
```

## Failure modes

| Situation | Correct behaviour |
|---|---|
| The source artifact names a file that isn't in the repo any more | Record the conflict in `open_questions`. Never plan against a path you couldn't find. |
| Nothing equivalent exists and this really needs a new module or service | Say so explicitly and ask first. It is a scope decision, not a step. |
| Work on the same files is already in flight elsewhere | Say what you found and confirm before planning over it. |
| No performance baseline exists for a latency-sensitive path | `[PENDING]`, evaluate against the threshold, and don't block on the missing baseline. Never estimate one. |
| The user says "skip the plan, just start coding" | Present the approach and wait for an explicit go-ahead; `plan.md` still needs the engineering lead's approval before implementation starts. |
